meta.gold_solution:
Solution:  
(I) $f'(x)=xe^{x}-ax=x(e^{x}-a)$  
When $a\leqslant 0$, $e^{x}-a > 0$, $\therefore x\in(-\infty,0)$, $f'(x)  0$, $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing;  
When $0  0$, $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing, $x\in(\ln a,0)$, $f'(x)  0$, $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing;  
(ii) When $a=1$, $\ln a=0$, $f'(x)=xe^{x}-ax=x(e^{x}-1)\geqslant 0$ always holds, $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing on $(-\infty,+\infty)$, with no decreasing interval;  
In summary, when $a\leqslant 0$, the monotonic increasing interval of $f(x)$ is $(0,+\infty)$, and the monotonic decreasing interval is $(-\infty,0)$;  
When $0  ax^{3}+x^{2}-(a-1)x$ always holds for $x\in(0,+\infty)$;  
That is, $e^{x}-ax^{2}-x-1 > 0$ always holds for $x\in(0,+\infty)$;  
Let $g(x)=e^{x}-ax^{2}-x-1(x > 0)$,  
$\therefore g'(x)=e^{x}-2ax-1=h(x)$; $\therefore h'(x)=e^{x}-2a$;  
(i) When $a\leqslant \frac {1}{2}$, $h'(x)=e^{x}-2a > 0$ always holds, $g'(x)$ is monotonically increasing on $(0,+\infty)$,  
$\therefore g'(x) > g'(0)=0$;  
$\therefore g(x)$ is monotonically increasing on $(0,+\infty)$;  
$\therefore g(x) > g(0)=0$, which is consistent with the problem statement;  
(ii) When $a > \frac {1}{2}$, let $h'(x)=0$ to get $x=\ln (2a)$;  
$\therefore x\in(0,\ln (2a))$, $h'(x) < 0$,  
$\therefore g'(x)$ is monotonically decreasing on $(0,\ln (2a))$;  
$\therefore x\in(0,\ln (2a))$, $g'(x) < g'(0)=0$;  
$\therefore g(x)$ is monotonically decreasing on $(0,\ln (2a))$,  
$\therefore x\in(0,\ln (2a))$, $g(x) < g(0)=0$, which is not consistent with the problem statement;  
In summary, the range of $a$ is $\boxed{(-\infty, \frac {1}{2}]}$.

