# Solution:

(a) Since the country can sell and buy goods on the world market, to achieve the consumption of the largest quantity of good sets, it needs to achieve the highest income from trade. For this, the country needs to use its resources as efficiently as possible. First, let's find the equation of the production possibilities frontier (PPF) of the country.

$Z_{X}+Z_{Y}=100$ according to the problem. At the same time, $Z_{X}=4 X^{2}, Z_{Y}=Y$.

Then the PPF equation is: $4 X^{2}+Y=100$.

The country's income, which uses its resources most efficiently in this case, is $T R=8 X+Y=8 X+100-4 X^{2}$. The maximum of this function, whose graph is a parabola opening downwards, is achieved at a production volume of $X_{\text {prod }}=1, Y_{\text {prod }}=100-4=96$.

The maximum income of the country is then $8 * 1+1 * 96=104$.

Considering the problem's condition, if the country consumes $\mathrm{X}$ units of good X, then it will consume $184 \mathrm{X} / 3$ units of good Y. Since the country will spend 104 monetary units on a set of goods in such a proportion, $8 X_{\text {cons }}+Y_{\text {cons }}=104$, where $Y_{\text {cons }}=184 X_{\text {cons }} / 3$ and the residents of the country will be able to consume $X_{\text {cons }}=1.5$ and $Y_{\text {cons }}=92$.

Let's graphically represent the obtained production and consumption volumes.

![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2024_05_06_30b7fccfc6f6ad7fdcc0g-07.jpg?height=654&width=717&top_left_y=204&top_left_x=310)

(b) Now let's check whether the country can benefit from an increase in the price of good X. If an increase in the price of this good will allow the country to consume a larger quantity of each good than in part (a), then it will benefit from such a price change.

First, let's find out how much of the good the country will produce if the price of good X increases by a factor of $\alpha$. Then the country's income, which uses its resources most efficiently in this case, is $T R=8 \alpha X+Y=8 \alpha X+100-4 X^{2}$. The maximum of this function, whose graph is a parabola opening downwards, is achieved at a production volume of $X_{\text {prod }}=\alpha, Y_{\text {prod }}=100-4 \alpha^{2}$.

The maximum income of the country is then

$8 \alpha * \alpha+1 *\left(100-4 \alpha^{2}\right)=4 \alpha^{2}+100$.

If the country's income with the change in the price of good X is greater than the expenses on the set of goods $X_{\text {cons }}=1.5, \quad Y_{\text {cons }}=92$, i.e., $4 \alpha^{2}+100>8 \alpha * 1.5+1 * 92$, then the country will benefit from an increase in the price of good X. The inequality will be satisfied for $\alpha>1$ only if $\alpha>2$, that is, if the price of good X increases by more than two times.

